equiv0\\pmod p.\n\\]\n(This also holds if \\(p\\) divides either base, since both exponents are positive.)\n\nConsequently, the fixed integer \\(a^k+b^k-c\\) is divisible by every prime \\(p>2015\\), and therefore is zero. Hence\n\\[\na+b=c=a^{P+1}+b^{P+1}.\n\\]\nFor positive integers \\(a,b\\), both \\(a^{P+1}-a\\) and \\(b^{P+1}-b\\) are nonnegative, vanishing only when their respective bases equal \\(1\\). Thus \\(a=b=1\\) and \\(c=2\\).\n\nConversely, this triple works because \\(a^n+b^n+n=n+2=n+c\\).\n\nIf “natural numbers” includes zero, the same proof instead gives \\(a,b\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and \\(c=a+b\\), and all four resulting triples work.\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{(a,b,c)=(1,1,2)}\\) for positive natural numbers; if zero is allowed, also \\(\\boxed{(0,0,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,1)}\\)."}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","mode":"standard","summary":null},"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":"default","store":false,"temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_logprobs":0,"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":142,"input_tokens_details":{"cache_write_tokens":0,"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2069,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1552},"total_tokens":2211},"user":null,"metadata":{}}
